\documentclass[11pt]{article}
\usepackage[margin=2.5cm]{geometry}
\usepackage{listings}

\lstset{basicstyle=\ttfamily\small, columns=fullflexible}

\title{Lab Report 4: Streaming Averages}
\author{COMP 208 --- Data Structures}
\date{May 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Objective}
This lab compares a naive recomputation of the moving average with an
incremental version that keeps a running sum over a sliding window.
The hypothesis is that the incremental version stays flat as the
window grows, while the naive version grows linearly with the window
width.

\section{Implementation}
The incremental implementation keeps the samples in a queue and
maintains the running sum as samples enter and leave the window:

\begin{lstlisting}
def moving_average(samples, width):
    total = 0.0
    window = []
    for value in samples:
        window.append(value)
        total += value
        if len(window) > width:
            total -= window.pop(0)
        yield total / len(window)

\section{Measurements}
Timing used one million samples with window widths from 16 to 4096.
The naive version slowed from 0.9~s to 41~s across that range, while
the incremental version stayed between 1.1~s and 1.3~s. The residual
growth in the incremental version comes from the queue operations,
not from the arithmetic.

\section{Discussion}
The measurements support the hypothesis. The incremental version is
slower than the naive one for the smallest window because of the
queue overhead, but the curves cross quickly and the asymptotic
behaviour dominates for any realistic window. One surprise was the
variance of the naive timings at large widths, which we attribute to
cache misses on the repeated scans.

\section{Conclusion}
Running aggregates should always be updated incrementally when the
window is large. The code above passes all unit tests in the lab
repository and matches the reference output for every provided
fixture.

\end{document}

